/-! # Compact results from the posted papers, batch 4 (FROZEN SPEC)

The frozen spec for `docs/briefs/cloud-spec-papers4.md`; see `docs/specs/PapersMore.md`.
Do not edit this file. Each theorem is proved by the matching `_aux` theorem in
`PapersMoreProofs.lean`. The manuscripts are the read-only copies
`docs/specs/inputs/papers/weil-nonsplit-g32-v0.16.tex` (G32 v0.16) and
`docs/specs/inputs/papers/weil-qsqrt3-v1.5.tex` (G34 v1.5); every citation is a printed number.
Item 6 (the rest) and item 15 (core statement 1) of `docs/specs/inputs/papers/lean-compact-list.md`.

It claims, for printed data transcribed exactly:

* G32 Appendix A: sixteen explicit products of the printed `r₁, r₂, x` (over any field of
  characteristic zero with a root `ω` of `ω² + ω + 1`) are linearly independent and span `M₄(K)`,
  so the algebra generated by `r₁, r₂, x` is `M₄(K)`; `r₁` and `r₂` alone do not generate it;
* G32 Subsection 5.2 and Appendix A: the printed `φ(s) = s²(s−3)⁴/((s−1)³(s+2)³)` has the printed
  derivative `3s(s−3)³(5s²−3s+4)/((s−1)⁴(s+2)⁴)` (as a polynomial identity and as a complex
  derivative off the poles), its finite critical points off the poles are `0`, `3` and the roots
  of `5s² − 3s + 4`, and the values of `φ` at those roots are exactly the roots of
  `729z² − 3674z + 5120`, which are not real;
* G34 Theorem 5.1, the finite core of the admissibility step, as an implication from stability:
  if the exponents of a supplier lie in `1, …, m − 1`, form a multiset stable under `α ↦ −α` and
  sum to `0` modulo `m`, then the exponents equal to `m/2` are even in number, `α` is simple, `s`
  is even, and the supplier is admissible as soon as `h = 2q − 2 + s > 0`.

It does not claim: that `ρ` is absolutely irreducible as a representation of a group, the order of
`G`, or anything about `G₃₂` as a group; physical rigidity, the local system `𝕃`, or that the
permutation monodromy of `φ` generates `S₆`; that the fibres of `φ` over `5` and `−7` are reduced or
that `φ(∞) = 1`; that real branch classes make the exponent multiset stable (the double-coset
argument), that the exponents sum to `0` modulo `m` (the product relation of the local
monodromies), Brauer's induction theorem, the homomorphism `a`, condition (L), Schoen's theorem or
any algebraicity. HC no distance; AV milestone no distance.
-/

